   "Source": "Resistivity 2.65e-8 ohm-m at 20 C: CRC Handbook of Chemistry and Physics, electrical resistivity of pure metals. Temperature coefficient 0.0039 /K and density 2700 kg/m3: CRC Handbook of Chemistry and Physics. Thermal conductivity k(T) and the shape of conductivity against temperature from 20 C to 626.85 C, the last tabulated row below the melting point (660.323 C, ITS-90): the pure bulk metal's recommended values, rho(T) from Desai, James and Ho, Electrical resistivity of aluminum and manganese, J. Phys. Chem. Ref. Data 13, 1131 (1984); k(T) from Ho, Powell and Liley, Thermal conductivity of the elements: a comprehensive review, J. Phys. Chem. Ref. Data 3, Supplement 1 (1974). The conductivity table is that rho(T) with a constant 7.41e-12 ohm-m added (Matthiessen's rule: an impurity adds a temperature-independent resistivity) so that it equals Sigma20 at 20 C; the slope with temperature is the pure metal's. Specific heat at 25 C: CRC Handbook of Chemistry and Physics.",
