   "Source": "Resistivity 2.44e-8 ohm-m and temperature coefficient 0.0034 /K at 20 C, as tabulated in university physics texts (e.g. Serway, Physics for Scientists and Engineers, table of resistivities). Representative of deposited gold: the CRC Handbook of Chemistry and Physics gives bulk gold 2.21e-8 ohm-m (4.5e7 S/m). Density: CRC Handbook of Chemistry and Physics. Thermal conductivity k(T) and the shape of conductivity against temperature from 20 C to 1026.85 C, the last tabulated row below the melting point (1064.18 C, ITS-90): the pure bulk metal's recommended values, rho(T) from Matula, Electrical resistivity of copper, gold, palladium, and silver, J. Phys. Chem. Ref. Data 8, 1147 (1979); k(T) from Ho, Powell and Liley, Thermal conductivity of the elements: a comprehensive review, J. Phys. Chem. Ref. Data 3, Supplement 1 (1974). The conductivity table is that rho(T) with a constant 2.24e-09 ohm-m added (Matthiessen's rule: an impurity adds a temperature-independent resistivity) so that it equals Sigma20 at 20 C; the slope with temperature is the pure metal's. Specific heat at 25 C: CRC Handbook of Chemistry and Physics.",
